the topic of stretching before  exercising has become one of growing  scrutiny one side says stretching as  mandatory while others think you're  crazy to even consider doing it back in  my high school days stretching before  you exercised was a paramount activity  you were practically required to do it  and if you didn't you were cast out like  a leper but enough of my awesome high  school stories  let's see who's actually right well the  truth is this is an incredibly complex  topic and both sides do have some  beneficial points but one side is  actually more right than the other  ideally stretching would technically  support and improve the contractility of  muscles making them more flexible and  increasing their strength  really the effects of stretching comes  down to the mechanics of how muscles  contract which by the way is understood  very well firstly all your muscles are  made up of large bundles of muscle  fibers these fibers are also bundles of  strands called myofibrils inside the  myofibrils are the smallest unit of  muscle tissue called sarcomeres  which is where the actual contraction  happens in the sarcomere there are two  proteins that cause contraction myosin  and actin here's how a muscle  contraction works an ATP molecule first  binds to myosin once bound the ATP is  converted into ADP which gives the  myosin protein energy to move the myosin  protein acts like a pair of hands and  grabs the actin protein pulling it  inward the ADP molecule unbinds in a new  ATP binds to the myosin causing it to  release the actin and the cycle repeats  every time you contract a muscle this  process is happening okay so how does  stretching apply to this muscle  contraction cycle well in 2013 a  research team at McGill University  wanted the test of stretching a  sarcomere before contraction had any  effect on the force that it generated  for the experiment the researchers used  sarcomeres extracted from the abdominal  muscles of a rather the sarcomeres were  placed in a solution that put them in a  relaxed non-contracting state then an  activator solution was added which made  the sarcomeres contract upon the first  contraction with no stretch beforehand  the sarcomere generated the force of 24  nanonewtons the sarcomeres were then  stretched with micro needles and placed  in the relaxed solution and then  reactivated again this time when the  sarcomere contracted it generated a  force of 37 nanonewtons a 21% increase  from the previous contraction  the experiment proved that actively  stretching prior to contraction causes  the sarcomere to produce a stronger long  lasting contraction this phenomenon is  known as residual force enhancement  there isn't the concrete understanding  of how it works but several theories  have been put forward one Theory  believes that stretching slows down the  rate of detachment of the myosin from  the actin recall in the contraction  cycle that when adp unbinds from the  myosin protein a new ATP binds to it  making it attached from the actin  protein however after stretching for  unknown reasons the ADP stays bound to  the myosin for a longer period of time  increasing the time the myosin binds to  the actin this longer attachment to the  actin is believed to be the cause that  generates more force the action may be  similar to pulling a boat closer to a  dock if you're in a boat and pulling a  rope attached to a dock say for four  seconds instead of two you'll generate  more pull force on the bow thereby  causing it to move of greater distance  we now know that stretching increases  the strength of muscle contraction but  what type of stretching actually does  this well in case you didn't know  there's actually two ways to stretch  statically and dynamically static  stretching is stretching in a fixed  position you extend a muscle with no  movement for say 20 or 30 seconds and  you're done  dynamic stretching is much different  because you're actually moving the  muscle past its range of motion several  times dynamic stretching could also be  called warming up static stretching is  becoming increasingly unpopular because  more and more scientific studies are  showing that it does not improve  performance and doesn't really make  sense biomechanically this is because  sports don't require you to stay in a  fixed extension for 30 seconds on the  other hand dynamic stretching is  becoming more popular because it makes  sense biomechanically as it mimics the  mechanical movement in sports raises  temperature of the tissue and increases  blood flow to the muscle if you're going  to stretch before sport it's best to use  dynamic stretching however static  stretching isn't completely useless it  does have one important purpose  flexibility consistently extending a  muscle does make it more flexible  thereby improving the athletes range of  motion don't believe me try to do a  split stretch right now yeah didn't  think so  static stretching makes the muscles more  flexible by training the nervous system  to accept greater and greater extensions  by constantly stretch  muscle fibers this doesn't mean it  prevents all injury just actions that  require high flexibility like ballet or  gymnastics we can agree that static  stretching is useful for flexibility and  dynamic is better for strength but do  either of these stretches prevent injury  evidence for injury prevention can be  hazy conflicting and outright confusing  numerous studies have shown that  stretching does not prevent injury  however these studies focus solely on  static stretching and not dynamic in the  study published in the British Journal  of Sports Medicine researchers followed  over 2300 participants for 12 weeks they  had the participants perform 30-second  static stretches before and after  physical activity researchers found that  static stretching before and after did  not produce any significant reduction in  injury however they did conclude that  stretching can reduce some minor  soreness as the stretch group reported  24% soreness and the non-stressed group  reported 32% so interesting but not  significant however a study in Sports  Medicine did theorize an important  correlation between stretching and the  sport being played sports that require  high intensity cycles of stretch  shortening actions like soccer and  football require a resilient well  maintained muscle tendon unit that can  store and release elastic energy at high  rates if players are relying on static  stretching which most Dell do they're  more likely to become injured on the  other hand sports with low intensity  elasticity demands like cycling and  swimming are prone to less injuries and  historically require less stretching  both of these scenarios can give the  appearance that stretching doesn't  prevent injury players who rely mostly  on static techniques and play high  elasticity demanding sports are more  prone to injury because they're using  the wrong stretch on the outside one  would say sea stretching doesn't prevent  injuries in low intensity sports where  there is less required stretching  there's also fewer injuries which would  also support the theory that stretching  is not related to injury prevention more  studies are showing dynamic stretching  does improve performance and strength  and some flexibility however injury  prevention is still difficult to prove  in conclusion stretching is beneficial  if you're going to play a sport or  workout use dynamic stretching because  it increases contraction force raises  muscle temperature and increases blood  flow to tissue if  doing gymnastics or dancing you'll need  flexibility so do static and dynamic  stretching finally it's hard to know for  sure if stretching prevents injury but  you're probably better off to stretch  regardless and click the bell to  subscribe you know  [Music]
 

